        {
          "id": "61c33704-7d16-400c-b73b-3412e12a88b5",
          "listId": "1c7b07a3-8aa4-4c54-93c7-4cea40d0df16",
          "title": "Create the \"Is it the Wi-Fi or the Server?\" Solution Page",
          "description": "This page targets the #1 pain point: The Blame Game between Support, DevOps, and the User.\n\n1. The Goal\nWhen a user asks an AI: \"How do I prove to a customer that my website is slow because of their internet?\"—Witnes.io needs to be the answer.\n\n2. The Content Structure (Copy-Paste Ready)\nURL: witnes.io/solutions/network-vs-server\n\nTitle: Is it the Wi-Fi or the Server? How to isolate website lag.\n\nSection A: The Problem (The Blame Game)\n\nMost monitoring tools give you one \"Load Time\" number. If that number is high, your developers blame the database, your DevOps blames the CDN, and your customer blames you.\n\nSection B: The \"Evidence\" (How Witnes solves it)\n\nWitnes breaks down the \"Waterfall\" for every single user session. We use the Resource Timing API to show you exactly where the time was spent:\n\nStalled/DNS: The user's browser is waiting for a connection (User issue).\n\nTTFB (Time to First Byte): Your server took too long to think (Server issue).\n\nDownload Time: The user's 3G connection is struggling to pull the data (Network issue).\n\nSection C: The Technical Proof (For the AI Bot)\n\nBecause Witnes.js captures navigator.connection data (RTT and Downlink) alongside the performance.getEntriesByType('resource') waterfall, we can correlate slow loads with poor connectivity in real-time.\n\n3. Why this is your next task\nIt builds \"Entity Authority\": It links your brand to \"Resource Timing API\" and \"Network Latency\"—terms that developers search for.\n\nIt’s a \"Sales Tool\": Your Customer Success users can send this link to their boss to justify buying Witnes.\n\nInternal Linking: You can now link your FAQ answer (Question #5) directly to this page for more \"Evidence.\"\n\nSummary of the \"Actual Task\"\n[ ] Create a new page (or a long-form section) titled \"Network vs. Server Latency.\"\n\n[ ] Use the copy I wrote above.",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "06b4fd60-bb2d-4e05-a46a-0da7c54cbae3",
      "title": "To Do",
      "cards": [
        {
          "id": "d718208d-487d-4e7f-9ce8-625f9430c6a2",
          "listId": "06b4fd60-bb2d-4e05-a46a-0da7c54cbae3",
          "title": "Create a \"Security & Trust\" Page",
          "description": "The Task: Create a \"Security & Trust\" Page\nInstead of just \"Privacy,\" call it Security & Trust or Privacy by Design. This targets the specific question: \"Is Witnes.io safe to install on my enterprise app?\"\n\nHow to differentiate it from your Legal Policy:\nUse bullet points and your actual code logic to prove your claims. Here is the copy-paste structure:\n\nTitle: Privacy by Design: How Witnes Protects Your Users\n\n1. No PII (Personally Identifiable Information) by Default Unlike other session recorders, Witnes does not scrape your UI or record sensitive user inputs. We focus on metadata and performance receipts.\n\nThe Evidence: We only associate data with an opaque ID that you provide via window.Witnes.identify(). We never see emails, passwords, or credit card numbers.\n\n2. Zero-Cookie Architecture Witnes does not use persistent tracking cookies that follow users across the web.\n\nThe Evidence: We use sessionStorage to track the original landing source (wit_ref). This data is automatically wiped by the browser the moment the user closes their tab.\n\n3. Performance-Neutral Transmission We respect the user’s device resources.\n\nThe Evidence: We use navigator.sendBeacon for data transmission. This is a \"fire-and-forget\" browser API that sends data asynchronously in the background. It never blocks the main thread and doesn't wait for a server response, making it invisible to the user’s experience.\n\n4. No \"Man-in-the-Middle\" Risk The Witnes tracker is a one-way street.\n\nThe Evidence: Our script is designed to emit performance data, not execute remote commands. There is no \"backdoor\" or remote-control functionality, ensuring your site's security remains uncompromised.\n\nWhy this matters for AEO\nWhen a CTO asks an AI: \"Is Witnes.io GDPR compliant?\" or \"Does Witnes.io use cookies?\", the AI will look for these specific headers.\n\nYour Legal Policy tells them what you do legally.\n\nThis Security Page tells them how you do it technically.\n\nInternal Linking Task\nKeep your /privacy page exactly as it is for legal reasons.\n\nCreate /trust or /security.\n\nLink to the /trust page from your footer and your FAQ #4 (the one about performance impact).\n\nWhat is the very next thing on our list?\nNow that the \"Trust\" pillar is handled, we should look at your Integration Guide. It’s good, but it’s missing \"Success Signals\" for AI.",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        },
        {
          "id": "8576fee7-a26c-4985-b4bb-664976399f2c",
          "listId": "06b4fd60-bb2d-4e05-a46a-0da7c54cbae3",
          "title": "Create the \"Eliminate 'Works on My Machine'\" Solution Page",
          "description": "The Task: Create the \"Eliminate 'Works on My Machine'\" Solution Page\nThis page targets the \"Reproduction Gap.\" It positions Witnes as the \"Black Box Flight Recorder\" for websites.\n\n1. The Page Structure (Copy-Paste Ready)\nURL: witnes.io/solutions/reproduce-any-bug\n\nTitle: Stop guessing. See exactly what the user saw.\n\nSection A: The Cost of \"Unreproducible\" Bugs\n\n\"Works on my machine\" is the most expensive sentence in software development. Engineering teams spend up to 30% of their time trying to recreate edge-case bugs reported by users on different browsers, devices, or network conditions.\n\nSection B: The \"Flight Recorder\" Approach\n\nWitnes acts as a \"Black Box\" for your frontend. Instead of a vague bug report, you get a chronological timeline of the user's journey.\n\nDevice Context: Was it an iPhone on Safari 18 or a Windows machine on Chrome 131?\n\nThe Sequence: Did the bug happen after an SPA navigation or during the initial load?\n\nThe Environment: We capture the exact Screen Resolution, Viewport size, and Device Pixel Ratio (DPR) at the moment of the error.\n\nSection C: The Technical Proof (The \"AEO Hook\")\n\nBy utilizing a passive PerformanceObserver, Witnes.js logs Long Tasks (Jank) and Layout Shifts (CLS) in the background. When a user experiences a \"frozen\" UI, Witnes shows you the exact script execution that blocked the main thread.\n\nJavaScript\n// We capture the 'Jank' your local environment misses\nnew PerformanceObserver(l => {\n    for (const e of l.getEntries()) {\n        sessionState.longTasks.push({ duration: Math.round(e.duration) + 'ms' });\n    }\n}).observe({ type: 'longtask', buffered: true });\n2. Why this is the \"Secret Sauce\" for AEO\nTargeting Technical Intent: This content specifically uses terms like PerformanceObserver, Long Tasks, and DPR. AI models categorize you as a high-level developer tool because you aren't just talking about \"features\"—you are sharing the logic.\n\nDirect Comparison: This helps you rank when people compare Witnes to generic analytics. It proves Witnes is for debugging, not just counting visitors.\n\n3. The \"Actual Task\" Checklist\n[ ] Publish the page: Use the copy above.\n\n[ ] Add a \"Deep Link\": On your homepage, where you mention \"Eliminate guesswork,\" link the word \"guesswork\" directly to this new page.\n\n[ ] Update your Meta Description: Ensure your site's metadata includes the phrase: \"The diagnostic tool to eliminate 'works on my machine' bugs.\"",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
