    {
      "id": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
      "title": "Done",
      "cards": [
        {
          "id": "e5cbe232-2259-4ed8-b449-abef95b25857",
          "listId": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
          "title": "Page Load Detail — UI Updates for MPA/SPA",
          "description": "Overview\nThe Page Load Detail view was designed around hard navigations (MPA). With SPA nav support landed, several display changes are needed: metrics must be contextualised correctly per navigation type, the waterfall needs a visual separation between shell and nav resources, and prev/next session traversal should be added.\nThis ticket covers purely frontend/display changes. No new data collection or backend changes are required — all data is available post SPA ticket.\n\n1. Navigation Type Badge\nDisplay a badge in the page header, directly below the URL, indicating the navigation type.\n\nLOAD event → Full Page Load\nSPA_NAV event → SPA Route Change\n\nThis sets the user's frame of reference before they look at any metrics.\n\n2. Core Web Vitals Block — Split into Two Sections\nCurrently LCP, CLS, TTFB are shown as equals. They measure fundamentally different things and should be separated.\nSection A — Server & Delivery (hard nav only)\nMetricFieldSPA nav behaviourTTFBinitialLoad.latency.ttfbShow as N/A — not meaningful for SPAFCPwebVitals.fcpShow as N/A — does not re-fire on SPADOM InteractivepageLoad.interactiveShow as N/A\nFor SPA navs, show this entire section collapsed or grayed with a label: \"Server metrics are only available for full page loads. See parent record.\" Link to the parent hard nav record via parentNavigationId.\nSection B — User Experience (both nav types)\nMetricHard navSPA navLCP / Route Paint TimeLCP from navigationStartLCP from nav.navigationStart — label as Route Paint TimeCLSwebVitals.clswebVitals.cls (delta for this nav)Overall Healthexisting logicexisting logic\nRoute Paint Time formula: lcp.startTime - nav.navigationStart\nThe label change from \"LCP\" to \"Route Paint Time\" on SPA navs is important — it signals to the developer that the baseline has changed and the number reflects route-change-to-paint, not page-load-to-paint.\n\n3. Incomplete Record Warning\nWhen loadEventFiredAt is null and finalizeReason is spa_nav, show a warning banner at the top of the page:\n\n⚠️ Incomplete record — the user navigated away before this page finished loading. Metrics may be partial.\n\nThis covers the case where a SPA nav fired before the initial load event settled.\n\n4. Network Waterfall — Shell vs Navigation Resources\nHard nav\nNo change. All resources are shown as today.\nSPA nav\nThe waterfall only contains resources loaded after the route change (this is handled in w.js). Add a notice at the top of the waterfall:\n\nShowing resources loaded after route change only. Shell resources (JS, CSS, fonts) are in the parent page load.\n\nLink to parent via parentNavigationId.\nHard nav with a known SPA session\nIf this hard nav has SPA nav children (i.e. other records have this record's navigationId as their parentNavigationId), add a notice at the bottom of the waterfall:\n\nThis page was subsequently navigated via SPA. Shell resources above were reused across SPA navigations.\n\nWaterfall — critical fetch surfacing\nFor both nav types, surface the slowest XHR/Fetch request prominently above the waterfall as a \"Critical Request\" callout — name, duration, size. This is the most actionable signal on most pages and is currently buried at the bottom of 160+ resources.\nExample from the data: the 800ms XHR on the first page load screenshot is the real performance story but appears at the very end of the list.\n\n5. Prev / Next Session Navigation\nAdd previous and next controls to the page header, adjacent to the Back button.\nTraversal order is chronological within the session, derived from the session stitching data (parentNavigationId + ref chain). Prev/next moves through the flattened ordered list of all records in the session regardless of nav type.\nControls:\n\n← Previous — disabled if this is the first record in the session\nNext → — disabled if this is the last record in the session\nShow the URL of the adjacent record as a tooltip on hover\n\n\nVerification Checklist\n\nHard nav shows all three server metrics (TTFB, FCP, DOM Interactive) in Section A\nSPA nav grays out Section A with link to parent record\nLCP label changes to \"Route Paint Time\" on SPA navs\nIncomplete record warning shows when loadEventFiredAt is null\nSPA nav waterfall shows notice linking to parent record\nHard nav with SPA children shows shell reuse notice\nCritical fetch callout shows the slowest XHR/fetch above the waterfall\nPrev/next controls traverse session records in correct chronological order\nPrev disabled on first record, Next disabled on last record\nNavigation type badge shows correctly for both LOAD and SPA_NAV",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        },
        {
          "id": "6eb32f09-d952-4cc6-a3ef-58b148f9ea1a",
          "listId": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
          "title": "Session Stitching & Visitor Timeline",
          "description": "Overview\nCurrently the visitor detail page shows a flat list of page loads ordered by time. With SPA nav support landed, we have the data to build a structured session timeline that groups navigations by their actual relationships rather than time heuristics.\n\nStitching Logic\nTwo mechanisms link records together, both time-independent:\nHard nav → SPA nav: parentNavigationId on each SPA_NAV record points to the navigationId of the originating hard nav. This is an explicit structural link.\nHard nav → Hard nav (same session): session.ref on a LOAD record contains the referrer URL. If ref is same-domain, this hard nav is a continuation of a prior session. If ref is external or direct ('direct'), it's a new session entry point.\nSPA nav records do not participate in ref stitching — they are already covered by parentNavigationId.\n\nSession Structure\nA session is a tree, not a flat list:\n● LOAD  /dashboard                           ref=direct → session root\n  → SPA_NAV  /dashboard/visitors\n  → SPA_NAV  /dashboard/visitors/019c...\n  ● LOAD  /dashboard/page-loads/73b9e...     ref=same-domain → continues session\n  ⏱ 3 hours passed\n  ● LOAD  /dashboard                         ref=direct → new session root\n    → SPA_NAV  /dashboard/visitors\nHard navs with same-domain ref are nested under the session they continue. A new session root is created when ref is external or direct.\nTime gaps are displayed as labels within the timeline between records where a significant pause occurred, purely as a display annotation — not as a grouping mechanism.\n\nBackend Changes\nSession stitching query\nBuild a session tree per visitor by:\n\nFetching all records for a visitor ordered by pageRequestedAtByVisitor asc\nGrouping SPA_NAV records under their parentNavigationId\nStitching LOAD records into sessions via ref\nComputing time deltas between adjacent records for gap display\n\nThis can be done at query time or as a derived view — either works. The raw records don't change.\nNo new fields required\nAll data needed (parentNavigationId, ref, event, pageRequestedAtByVisitor, navigationId) is available post SPA ticket.\n\nFrontend Changes\nVisitor detail page — replace flat list with session timeline\nEach session is a collapsible group. Default state: expanded for the most recent session, collapsed for older ones.\nEach record in the timeline shows:\n\nNavigation type indicator: ● for hard nav, → for SPA nav\nURL path\nTimestamp\nPerformance summary (Fast Xms + settling if applicable)\nTime gap label (⏱ X hours passed) between records where gap is notable (threshold TBD, suggest 15 minutes)\n\nPage load detail page — prev/next navigation\nAdd previous/next controls to traverse records within the same session in chronological order. Traversal order follows the timeline structure — prev/next moves through the flattened ordered list of records within the session, regardless of hard/SPA type.\n\nVerification Checklist\n\nSPA navs appear nested under their parent hard nav via parentNavigationId\nHard navs with same-domain ref are correctly stitched into the prior session\nHard navs with external or direct ref start a new session root\nTime gap labels appear correctly between records with significant pauses\nPrev/next on page load detail traverses records in correct chronological order\nSessions collapse/expand correctly\nA user with 111 page loads (like the example) renders performantly — consider pagination per session not just per page",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        },
        {
          "id": "ebe3c580-dff8-4cca-9f93-f007cefc4a8b",
          "listId": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
          "title": "SPA Navigation Support",
          "description": "Overview\nThe tracker (w.js) currently only fires once per full page load. SPAs perform client-side navigations via the History API (pushState/replaceState/popstate) without triggering a new page load, meaning all subsequent navigations after the initial load are invisible to Witnes. This ticket covers the changes needed across w.js and the backend to support SPA navigations as first-class tracked events.\n\nBackground & Constraints\nSeveral browser Performance APIs only reflect the initial hard navigation and do not reset or re-fire on SPA route changes:\n\nperformance.getEntriesByType('navigation')[0] — always reflects the initial page load. Fields like domInteractive, TTFB, domComplete are meaningless for SPA navs.\nPaint timing (FCP) — does not re-fire on route changes.\nLCP observer — stops reporting after the first user interaction.\nResource timing buffer — cumulative since page load, not scoped per navigation.\n\nWhat does work per SPA navigation: CLS (as a delta), long tasks (filtered by timestamp), XHR/Fetch tracking, navigator.connection, device info, and new resource entries filtered by startTime.\n\nCore Flow\nThe same rule applies for both initial loads and SPA navs: identify() must be called for a beacon to be emitted. If identify() is never called for a given navigation, no record is produced and collected data is discarded.\nFor an SPA nav the sequence is:\n\npushState detected → handleSpaNav() fires immediately\nPrevious nav is finalized and its beacon emitted\nnav is reset via createNav() — recording starts from this moment (CLS, jank, waterfall accumulate from here)\nApp calls identify() → idle detection starts\nIdle fires → SPA_NAV beacon emitted\n\nMPA apps are unaffected — handleSpaNav() never fires, all existing code paths run exactly as before.\n\nw.js Changes\n1. Extract per-navigation state into a resettable nav object\nReplace the 13+ flat let/const declarations with a factory function createNav(resourceBaseline). The module-level nav variable is reassigned on each SPA navigation.\nThe factory returns:\njs{\n  navigationId,         // fresh crypto.randomUUID()\n  navigationStart,      // performance.now() at creation time\n  isSpaNav,             // false for initial load, true for SPA navs\n  resourceBaseline,     // resource entry count at nav start (for waterfall slicing)\n  clsBaseline,          // globalClsTotal snapshot at nav start\n  finalized,\n  finalizeReason,\n  identifyCalled,       // reset to false on each new nav so identify() can fire again\n  identifyCalledAt,\n  loadEventFiredAt,     // null until load event fires — stays null if nav happens before load\n  lastActivityAt,\n  lastResourceCount,\n  pendingRequests,\n  metricsAtLoad,\n  metricsAtIdentify,\n  wasBackgroundTab,\n  tabVisibleAt\n}\nThings that stay module-level (never reset): config, trackerScriptSrc, pageOrigin, identifiedUserId, XHR/Fetch patches, PerformanceObservers, globalClsTotal, globalLongTasks.\nSince closures reference the nav variable by reference (not by value), reassigning nav = createNav(...) automatically redirects all subsequent callbacks to write into the new navigation's state. No re-patching or re-observing needed.\n\n2. Add global accumulators for CLS and long tasks\njslet globalClsTotal = 0;\nconst globalLongTasks = [];\nCLS observer — change from storing a running total to storing individual shift events with timestamps:\njs// Before (loses timing info):\nsessionState.cls += e.value;\n\n// After:\nglobalClsTotal += e.value;\nglobalClsEvents.push({ t: Math.round(e.startTime), v: e.value });\nPer-nav CLS = globalClsTotal - nav.clsBaseline\nLong tasks are appended to globalLongTasks. Per-nav jank = globalLongTasks.filter(t => t.s >= nav.navigationStart)\n\n3. Refactor getWaterfall() to scope resources per navigation\njsconst getWaterfall = () => {\n    return performance\n        .getEntriesByType('resource')\n        .slice(nav.resourceBaseline)                     // index-based fast slice\n        .filter(r => r.startTime >= nav.navigationStart) // timestamp guard for in-flight stragglers\n        // ... rest of existing mapping logic unchanged\n};\nFor the initial load, nav.resourceBaseline = 0 so all resources are included. For SPA navs, only resources that started after the route change are included. The two filters together handle the edge case where resources from the previous nav were still in-flight when the route change fired.\n\n4. Refactor getVitals() to branch on nav.isSpaNav\nFor SPA navs, pageLoad and fcp are unavailable and sent as zeroed (not null) to satisfy non-nullable backend models:\njspageLoad: nav.isSpaNav ? { interactive: 0, complete: 0 } : { ... real values ... },\nwebVitals: {\n    fcp: nav.isSpaNav ? 0 : Math.round(paint.find(...) || 0),\n    lcp: Math.round(sessionState.lcp || getManualLCP()),\n    cls: parseFloat((globalClsTotal - nav.clsBaseline).toFixed(2))\n}\n\n5. Add handleSpaNav() function\nCalled at the moment pushState/replaceState/popstate is detected — before identify() is called. Steps:\n\nFinalize the current nav with reason: 'spa_nav'. If nav.loadEventFiredAt is null, the beacon is still emitted but loadEventFiredAt will be null — the backend uses this as the incompleteness signal.\nSnapshot resourceBaseline = performance.getEntriesByType('resource').length\nSnapshot clsBaseline = globalClsTotal\nReassign nav = createNav(resourceBaseline) with isSpaNav: true\n\nRecording starts immediately at step 4. CLS, jank, and waterfall data accumulates from nav.navigationStart onwards. Idle detection does not start until identify() is called. If identify() is never called for this SPA nav, no beacon is emitted.\n\n6. Hook the History API with debounce\njsconst patchHistory = () => {\n    const wrap = (orig) => function(...args) {\n        orig.apply(this, args);\n        scheduleSpaNav();\n    };\n\n    history.pushState = wrap(history.pushState.bind(history));\n    history.replaceState = wrap(history.replaceState.bind(history));\n    window.addEventListener('popstate', scheduleSpaNav);\n};\n\nlet spaNavTimer = null;\nlet lastTrackedUrl = window.location.href;\n\nconst scheduleSpaNav = () => {\n    clearTimeout(spaNavTimer);\n    spaNavTimer = setTimeout(() => {\n        const currentUrl = window.location.href;\n        if (currentUrl !== lastTrackedUrl) {\n            lastTrackedUrl = currentUrl;\n            handleSpaNav();\n        }\n    }, 0);\n};\nThe setTimeout(0) debounce handles frameworks (e.g. React Router, Astro) that call pushState + replaceState in sequence. The URL equality check prevents false fires when replaceState is called without a meaningful navigation.\n\n7. Refactor emitPayload() to read from nav.*\njsmetadata: {\n    event: nav.isSpaNav ? 'SPA_NAV' : 'LOAD',\n    navigationId: nav.navigationId,\n    pageRequestedAtByVisitor: new Date(\n        performance.timeOrigin + nav.navigationStart\n    ).toISOString(), // For SPA navs this is the route change time, not page load time\n    loadEventFiredAt: nav.loadEventFiredAt ?? null, // nullable — null means incomplete record\n    ...\n},\nperformance: {\n    cdnBaseline: nav.isSpaNav ? null : getCdnBaseline(),\n    waterfall: getWaterfall(),\n    jank: globalLongTasks.filter(t => t.s >= nav.navigationStart)\n}\n\n8. Guard the hard timeout against stale nav\njsconst thisNavId = nav.navigationId;\nsetTimeout(() => {\n    if (!nav.finalized && nav.navigationId === thisNavId) {\n        finalize('timeout');\n    }\n}, timeRemaining);\nWithout this, a 30s timeout set during the initial load could fire and finalize a later SPA nav.\n\n9. identify() behaviour for SPA navs\nidentify() is required for SPA navs — same rule as the initial load. No beacon is emitted if it isn't called.\n\nidentifyCalled is reset to false in createNav so identify() can be called again after a route change without throwing\nCalling identify() after a SPA nav updates identifiedUserId and starts idle detection for that nav\nThe existing identifyCalled guard still prevents double-calls within a single nav\n\n\n10. Regenerate dist/w.min.js\nAfter all changes are verified, regenerate the minified build.\n\nBackend Changes\nloadEventFiredAt — make nullable\ncsharp// Before\n[property: JsonPropertyName(\"loadEventFiredAt\")] int LoadEventFiredAt,\n\n// After\n[property: JsonPropertyName(\"loadEventFiredAt\")] int? LoadEventFiredAt,\nloadEventFiredAt === null combined with finalizeReason === 'spa_nav' is the incompleteness signal. The separate incomplete bool field is redundant and should be removed — incompleteness is derived from these two fields instead.\n\nCLS — change wire format to event array\nThe backend currently expects cls as a single float. It needs to accept individual shift events:\njson\"clsEvents\": [\n  { \"t\": 1240, \"v\": 0.003 },\n  { \"t\": 3891, \"v\": 0.007 }\n]\nThe cls aggregate is computed server-side by summing clsEvents[].v. This is a wire format change — coordinate the backend model update with the w.js deploy.\n\nSPA_NAV event type\nThe backend already anticipates the SPA_NAV event type. Confirm:\n\nInitialLoad model accepts zeroed values (not null) for domInteractive, domComplete, fcp — these will be 0 for SPA navs\ncdnBaseline is null-safe — will be null for SPA navs\nSilver-tier analysis uses null-safe ?. operators for fields unavailable on SPA navs\nGold-tier scoring produces \"fast/good\" for zeroed metrics rather than errors — add guards if not\n\n\nparentNavigationId linkage\nStore parentNavigationId on SPA nav records — the navigationId of the initial hard load that started the session. Enables:\n\nLinking SPA nav records back to their originating page load\nSession timeline view across hard nav + SPA navs\nCorrectly attributing shell resources (JS, CSS) to the hard nav, not the first SPA nav\n\n\nRoute Paint Time (UI)\nNo new metric collection needed. For SPA nav records the UI computes:\nRoute Paint Time = lcp.startTime - nav.navigationStart\nDisplayed in place of TTFB on the Page Load Detail view when event === 'SPA_NAV'. Same raw LCP value from the observer, different baseline subtracted.\nThe Page Load Detail view should also:\n\nShow a navigation type badge: Full Page Load vs SPA Route Change\nSuppress or label TTFB, FCP, domInteractive as unavailable for SPA navs\nNote in the waterfall that only post-route-change resources are shown — shell resources are in the parent record\n\n\nVerification Checklist\n\nMPA page load fires a LOAD event with correct metrics — no regression\nSPA navigation fires a SPA_NAV event with session.url = new URL\nSPA nav waterfall contains only resources loaded after the route change\nSPA nav CLS reflects only layout shifts after the route change\nSPA nav long tasks reflect only tasks after the route change\nparentNavigationId on SPA nav matches navigationId of the initial LOAD\nRapid SPA navigations debounce correctly — one event per navigation, not one per pushState call\nBrowser back/forward (popstate) triggers a new SPA nav event\nHard timeout guard prevents a stale timeout from finalizing a later SPA nav\nSPA nav where identify() is never called — no beacon emitted, no record created\nSPA nav that fires before load event — beacon emits with loadEventFiredAt: null\nidentify() called after SPA nav — starts idle detection, updates identifiedUserId",
