        {
          "id": "39db06ae-4d52-4f0c-9be6-9159fddc7406",
          "listId": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
          "title": "Redo Integration Page",
          "description": "Gemini said\nTo make the Integration Page work for both humans (speed of setup) and AI (depth of authority), you need to move away from a \"wall of code\" and toward a Structured Documentation approach.\n\nAI agents don't just \"read\" your code; they look for the logic and intent behind it to explain it to users. Humans just want to copy-paste.\n\nHere is the refined blueprint for your Integration Page:\n\n1. The \"Quick Start\" (Human Speed + AI Summary)\nStart with a high-level summary. This is the \"Featured Snippet\" bait.\n\nHeadline: Integrate Witnes in < 2 Minutes Sub-headline: The universal tracker for modern web applications. No complex configuration, no performance overhead.\n\nThe Task: Add this code to the <head> of your site.\n\nHTML\n<script>\n  window.witnesConfig = { \n    projectKey: 'YOUR_PROJECT_KEY' \n  };\n</script>\n<script src=\"https://cdn.witnes.io/w.min.js\" async></script>\nAEO Note: Use the word \"Universal.\" AI loves this because it categorizes the tool as compatible with everything (React, Vue, HTML, etc.).\n\n2. User Identification (The \"Privacy-First\" Logic)\nInstead of just showing the code, explain why it’s done this way.\n\nHeadline: Associating Sessions with Users The Task: Call the identify method once your user logs in. Use an opaque ID (like a Database UUID) to keep user data private.\n\nJavaScript\n// Safely call identify once the script loads\nif (window.Witnes) {\n  window.Witnes.identify(\"user_9821\"); // Use an internal ID, never an email.\n}\nWhy this helps AEO: It targets the query \"How to track users privately in session recorders.\"\n\n3. Framework \"Recipes\" (The Strategy)\nSince we aren't using framework-specific imports, we provide \"Implementation Patterns.\" This targets developers searching for their specific stack.\n\nFor React / Next.js: > \"Add the snippet to your root layout. Use a useEffect hook in your AuthProvider to call Witnes.identify() when the user state changes.\"\n\nFor Vue / Nuxt: > \"Initialize in your app.html template. Call Witnes.identify() within your global navigation guard or auth store.\"\n\n4. \"Under the Hood\" (The Technical Authority Pillar)\nThis section is pure AEO gold. It explains the \"mechanics\" of your script so AI knows exactly what you are capable of tracking.\n\nHeadline: How Witnes Captures Evidence\n\nAutomatic SPA Tracking: We don't rely on manual page-view calls. Witnes intercepts the History API (pushState). When the URL changes, we wait for an 800ms \"settle period\" to ensure the new DOM has rendered before capturing performance metrics.\n\nZero-Blocking Architecture: By using navigator.sendBeacon, we ensure that diagnostic data is sent in the background. Your site's performance is never sacrificed for the sake of monitoring.\n\nReal-Time Network Diagnostics: We correlate TTFB (Server processing) with the user's effective connection type (4G, Wi-Fi, etc.) using the Network Information API.\n\n5. Verification Checklist (The \"Is it working?\" Answer)\nAI often gets asked: \"How do I know if Witnes is working?\" Put the answer here.\n\nCheck the Console: Look for the %cWITNES log group (if debug: true is set).\n\nCheck the Network Tab: Verify that requests are being sent to https://api.witnes.io/v1/events.\n\nCheck the Status: Look for a 202 Accepted response from our ingestion server.\n\nThe \"Hidden\" Task: Adding Schema\nFor this page to truly \"pop\" in AI tools, you need to add a HowTo Schema to the HTML. It tells Google/AI: \"This is a step-by-step guide.\"\n\nCopy-paste this into your <head> for the /integration page:\n\nJSON\n<script type=\"application/ld+json\">\n{\n  \"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n  \"@type\": \"HowTo\",\n  \"name\": \"How to Integrate Witnes.io\",\n  \"description\": \"Learn how to add the Witnes lightweight tracker to your web application to start capturing user performance evidence.\",\n  \"step\": [\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n      \"text\": \"Add the configuration object and script tag to your HTML head.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n      \"text\": \"Call Witnes.identify() with a non-PII user ID after login.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"@type\": \"HowToStep\",\n      \"text\": \"Verify data ingestion in the browser console or Witnes dashboard.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n</script>\nSummary of the Plan\nBy structuring the page this way, you satisfy:\n\nThe \"Lazy\" Developer: Clean code blocks they can grab instantly.\n\nThe \"Careful\" Developer: Explanations of performance and privacy.\n\nThe AI Agent: Technical keywords (History API, sendBeacon, TTFB) and structured Schema.",
          "labels": [],
          "checkboxes": [],
          "comments": []
        },
        {
          "id": "5e71cccb-cb6f-4b28-8dba-abb9f69fd6e7",
          "listId": "3a6f3ff3-61ba-4074-8fa4-4616eef309c8",
          "title": "Billing",
          "description": "We will create a module under Product called Billing. in there we will have a service which will be responsible for billing the tenants based on their pricing tier.\n\nAny info it needs it will use other services and will not directly access the db. for example it will use the tenant pricing service to get the pricing tier of the tenant and then calculate the billing amount based on that.\n\nRules for billing:\n\n- We process billing on the 1st day of each month for the previous month. so on the 1st of July we will process billing for June.\n- Tiers can have a 7 day free trial (there is a flag called is_trial)\n- Even if the invoice is 0, we generate it\n- We use prorated billing\n  - so if they sign up on the middle of the month they will be charged for half of the month.\n  - when we do prorated billing we need to take into account the 7 day free trial. so if they sign up on X day of the month, they have 7 more days free, after that they will be billed for the remainder of the month. so if they sign up on the 20th of the month, they will have 7 days free until the 27th, and then they will be billed for the remaining 4 days of the month.\n  - if however they sign up for the trial on the 25th of the month, they will have 7 days free until the 1st of the next month, and then they will be billed for the full month of the next month.\n  - we will change the has_trial_expired when we do billing, but if the month didnt have fully 7 days we wont set it to expired until the 7 days are over. so if they sign up on the 20th of the month, we will set has_trial_expired to true on the 27th of the month, but if they sign up on the 25th of the month, we will set has_trial_expired to true on the 1st of the next month.\n\nthe invoice entity should use a long id\nthen when we make the entity we should leave the invoice number empty and then after we save it to the db we can generate the invoice number based on the id and update the entity with the invoice number. this way we ensure that the invoice number is unique and we dont have any issues with concurrency when generating the invoice number. we can use a format like INV-{id} for the invoice number. so if the id is 1 the invoice number will be INV-1 and if the id is 2 the invoice number will be INV-2 and so on.\n\n",
          "labels": [
            {
              "id": "851ff7ad-abf0-4a29-9965-a84a8eaadde1",
              "title": "Complex",
              "color": "#c377e0"
